ingles","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":212684728441441,"name":"Billboard Year-End 1967","rank":61,"scaruffi":null},{"id":132335824845498,"name":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","rank":61,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":2}}],"members":[],"member_of":[],"genres":[],"album_genres":[{"id":247802226836647,"type":"genre","name":"Brill Building","n":6},{"id":100106212412601,"type":"genre","name":"Teen Pop","n":3}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":266237870248406,"name":"Girl groups y Shangri-Las","book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","start":1960,"end":1966,"desc":"Antes del beat, el estilo de los grupos femeninos dominó las listas americanas desde que las Shirelles llegaron al número 1 con «Will You Love Me Tomorrow» (1961): discos afilados, bailables y bien observados. «My Boyfriend’s Back» de las Angels y «You Don’t Own Me» de Lesley Gore eran declaraciones chocantes. Tras la invasión británica, los Shangri-Las de Red Bird lo prolongaron un par de años: chicas duras de Queens a las que George «Shadow» Morton dejaba ser ellas mismas en sinfonías de 45 rpm con gaviotas y motos, sobre romances condenados y tragedias familiares, «como la chica corriente». Su último single en Red Bird, «Past, Present and Future» (mayo de 1966), sobre la «Sonata Claro de luna», es un examen desolado de un desastre amoroso.","color":"#38bdf8","family":"Pop de estudio: Spector, Beach Boys, Meek","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#girl-groups"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":277424529226218,"artist":"Lesley Gore","artist_id":10821678654930,"title":"You Don’t Own Me","year":1963,"ess":0,"yt":"OYB1rbL8EHo","kind":null,"book":{"id":1,"title":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/"},"scene":"Girl groups y Shangri-Las","scene_id":266237870248406,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#girl-groups","chapters":["Mayo: Walkin’ My Cat Named Dog: la mística de la feminidad y la independencia femenina"],"pages":[[1,126],[1,132],[1,151],[1,469]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":126,"text":"…1960s, songs by female artists tended to be about boys and affairs of the heart. In this climate the Angels’ vengeful ‘My Boyfriend’s Back’ and Lesley Gore’s declaration of independence ‘You Don’t Own Me’ were shocking declarations. The Shangri-Las pushed these themes into more realistic scenarios. ‘I…"},{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":132,"text":"…triumph was matched with controversy and collapse. Dusty began by singing a mixture of R&B, Motown, showbiz ballads and girl group records like Lesley Gore’s ‘You Don’t Own Me’. In 1964, she focused on songs by Burt Bacharach and Hal David. Her first cover was of ‘Wishin’ and Hopin’’ – a Dionne Warwick…"},{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":151,"text":"…Louie”’ as forming part of the Factory soundtrack, along with Billy Name’s opera records. The next year, he remembers working at the Factory to Lesley Gore singing ‘You Don’t Own Me’ and Dionne Warwick’s ‘A House Is Not a Home’. This formed an aural collage, a clashing sound environment. Remembering his…"}],"podcast":[],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":26719352385239,"type":"song","name":"It's My Party","work_year":1963,"list_id":157497903047680,"list":"Billboard Hot 100 nº1 · 1960s","src":"singles","rank":91,"year":1963,"note":"2 semanas","yt":"mtCIdpnQoWk","scaruffi":null},{"id":26719352385239,"type":"song","name":"It's My Party","work_year":1963,"list_id":271704385319129,"list":"Billboard Year-End 1963","src":"singles","rank":23,"year":1963,"note":"","yt":"mtCIdpnQoWk","scaruffi":null},{"id":81268409980046,"type":"song","name":"Judy's Turn to Cry","work_year":1963,"list_id":271704385319129,"list":"Billboard Year-End 1963","src":"singles","rank":72,"year":1963,"note":"","yt":"mw9ZQVFS-vA","scaruffi":null}